2026-10-20T21:06:14.1490470Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1491045Z +               '* OWASP: [Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1491453Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-327](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1491852Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-328](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492244Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-916](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/916.html).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492322Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492501Z +           id: 'py/weak-sensitive-data-hashing',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492682Z +           name: 'py/weak-sensitive-data-hashing',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492788Z +           properties: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1492948Z +             'problem.severity': 'warning',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1493229Z +             'security-severity': '7.5',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1493636Z +             description: 'Using broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithms can compromise security.',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494044Z +             id: 'py/weak-sensitive-data-hashing',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494197Z +             kind: 'path-problem',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494538Z +             name: 'Use of a broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithm on sensitive data',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494671Z +             precision: 'high',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494763Z +             tags: [
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1494870Z +               'security',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495012Z +               'external/cwe/cwe-327',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495150Z +               'external/cwe/cwe-328',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495293Z +               'external/cwe/cwe-916'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495377Z +             ]
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495454Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495587Z +           shortDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1495925Z +             text: 'Use of a broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithm on sensitive data'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496007Z +           }
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496090Z +         },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496312Z +         {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496456Z +           defaultConfiguration: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496567Z +             enabled: true,
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496689Z +             level: 'warning'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496770Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1496892Z +           fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1497325Z +             text: 'Leaving the SSL/TLS version unspecified may result in an insecure default protocol being used.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1497407Z +           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1497497Z +           help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1497748Z +             markdown: '# Default version of SSL/TLS may be insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1498516Z +               'The `ssl.wrap_socket` function defaults to an insecure version of SSL/TLS when no specific protocol version is specified. This may leave the connection vulnerable to attack.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1498612Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1498708Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1498832Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1500299Z +               'Ensure that a modern, strong protocol is used. All versions of SSL, and TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are known to be vulnerable to attacks. Using TLS 1.2 or above is strongly recommended. If no explicit `ssl_version` is specified, the default `PROTOCOL_TLS` is chosen. This protocol is insecure because it allows TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 and so should not be used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1501086Z -               'Modify the regular expression to remove the ambiguity, or ensure that the strings matched with the regular expression are short enough that the time-complexity does not matter.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1501174Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1501255Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1501371Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502081Z +               'The following code shows two different ways of setting up a connection using SSL or TLS. They are both potentially insecure because the default version is used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502561Z -               'Consider this use of a regular expression, which removes all leading and trailing whitespace in a string:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502649Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502747Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502855Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1502984Z +               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1503113Z +               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1503199Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1503421Z +               '# Using the deprecated ssl.wrap_socket method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1503600Z +               'ssl.wrap_socket(socket.socket())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1503697Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1504051Z +               '# Using SSLContext\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1504363Z +               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1504468Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1504667Z -               're.sub(r"^\\s+|\\s+$", "", text) # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1504759Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1505448Z +               'Both of the cases above should be updated to use a secure protocol instead, for instance by specifying `ssl_version=PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2` as a keyword argument.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1507131Z -               'The sub-expression `"\\s+$"` will match the whitespace characters in `text` from left to right, but it can start matching anywhere within a whitespace sequence. This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a whitespace character. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each whitespace sequence once per whitespace character in the sequence.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1507220Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1508790Z +               'The latter example can also be made secure by modifying the created context before it is used to create a connection. Therefore it will not be flagged by this query. However, if a connection is created before the context has been secured (for example, by setting the value of `minimum_version`), then the code should be flagged by the query `py/insecure-protocol`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1510017Z -               'This ultimately means that the time cost of trimming a string is quadratic in the length of the string. So a string like `"a b"` will take milliseconds to process, but a similar string with a million spaces instead of just one will take several minutes.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1510100Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1510538Z +               'Note that `ssl.wrap_socket` has been deprecated in Python 3.7. The recommended alternatives are:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1511680Z -               'Avoid this problem by rewriting the regular expression to not contain the ambiguity about when to start matching whitespace sequences. For instance, by using a negative look-behind (`^\\s+|(?<!\\s)\\s+$`), or just by using the built-in strip method (`text.strip()`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1511770Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1512091Z +               '* `ssl.SSLContext` - supported in Python 2.7.9, 3.2, and later versions\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1512545Z +               '* `ssl.create_default_context` - a convenience function, supported in Python 3.4 and later versions.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1514002Z +               'Even when you use these alternatives, you should ensure that a safe protocol is used. The following code illustrates how to use flags (available since Python 3.2) or the \\`minimum_version\\` field (favored since Python 3.7) to restrict the protocols accepted when creating a connection.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1514885Z -               'Note that the sub-expression `"^\\s+"` is **not** problematic as the `^` anchor restricts when that sub-expression can start matching, and as the regular expression engine matches from left to right.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1514975Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1515061Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1515186Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1515886Z -               'As a similar, but slightly subtler problem, consider the regular expression that matches lines with numbers, possibly written using scientific notation:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1515984Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516091Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516221Z +               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516302Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516501Z +               '# Using flags to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516669Z +               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1516922Z +               'context.options |= ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1 | ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1_1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1517019Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1517413Z +               '# Declaring a minimum version to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1517610Z +               'context = ssl.create_default_context()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1517851Z +               'context.minimum_version = ssl.TLSVersion.TLSv1_2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1517953Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1518097Z -               '^0\\.\\d+E?\\d+$ # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1518187Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1519128Z -               'The problem with this regular expression is in the sub-expression `\\d+E?\\d+` because the second `\\d+` can start matching digits anywhere after the first match of the first `\\d+` if there is no `E` in the input string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1519216Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1519352Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1519801Z +               '* Wikipedia: [ Transport Layer Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1520315Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ class ssl.SSLContext](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLContext).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1520788Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ ssl.wrap_socket](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.wrap_socket).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1521542Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ notes on context creation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#functions-constants-and-exceptions).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1522085Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ notes on security considerations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl-security).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1522495Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-327](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1522722Z +             text: '# Default version of SSL/TLS may be insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1523492Z +               'The `ssl.wrap_socket` function defaults to an insecure version of SSL/TLS when no specific protocol version is specified. This may leave the connection vulnerable to attack.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1524715Z -               'This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a digit. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each digit sequence once per digit in the sequence, again leading to a quadratic time complexity.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1524814Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1525553Z -               'To make the processing faster, the regular expression should be rewritten such that the two `\\d+` sub-expressions do not have overlapping matches: `^0\\.\\d+(E\\d+)?$`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1525641Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1525791Z +               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1527258Z +               'Ensure that a modern, strong protocol is used. All versions of SSL, and TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are known to be vulnerable to attacks. Using TLS 1.2 or above is strongly recommended. If no explicit `ssl_version` is specified, the default `PROTOCOL_TLS` is chosen. This protocol is insecure because it allows TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 and so should not be used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1527349Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1527449Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1527564Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1528270Z +               'The following code shows two different ways of setting up a connection using SSL or TLS. They are both potentially insecure because the default version is used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1529581Z -               'Sometimes it is unclear how a regular expression can be rewritten to avoid the problem. In such cases, it often suffices to limit the length of the input string. For instance, the following regular expression is used to match numbers, and on some non-number inputs it can have quadratic time complexity:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1529667Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1529764Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1529876Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1529999Z +               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1530279Z +               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1530365Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1530587Z +               '# Using the deprecated ssl.wrap_socket method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1530774Z +               'ssl.wrap_socket(socket.socket())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1530865Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1531009Z +               '# Using SSLContext\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1531176Z +               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1531267Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1531629Z -               "match = re.search(r'^(\\+|-)?(\\d+|(\\d*\\.\\d*))?(E|e)?([-+])?(\\d+)?$', str) \n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1531723Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1532407Z +               'Both of the cases above should be updated to use a secure protocol instead, for instance by specifying `ssl_version=PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2` as a keyword argument.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1533446Z -               'It is not immediately obvious how to rewrite this regular expression to avoid the problem. However, you can mitigate performance issues by limiting the length to 1000 characters, which will always finish in a reasonable amount of time.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1533665Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1535495Z +               'The latter example can also be made secure by modifying the created context before it is used to create a connection. Therefore it will not be flagged by this query. However, if a connection is created before the context has been secured (for example, by setting the value of `minimum_version`), then the code should be flagged by the query `py/insecure-protocol`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1535598Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1536045Z +               'Note that `ssl.wrap_socket` has been deprecated in Python 3.7. The recommended alternatives are:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1536140Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1536464Z +               '* `ssl.SSLContext` - supported in Python 2.7.9, 3.2, and later versions\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1536918Z +               '* `ssl.create_default_context` - a convenience function, supported in Python 3.4 and later versions.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538153Z +               'Even when you use these alternatives, you should ensure that a safe protocol is used. The following code illustrates how to use flags (available since Python 3.2) or the \\`minimum_version\\` field (favored since Python 3.7) to restrict the protocols accepted when creating a connection.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538252Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538348Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538455Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538584Z +               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538668Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1538858Z +               '# Using flags to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539022Z +               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539277Z +               'context.options |= ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1 | ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1_1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539427Z -               'if len(str) > 1000:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539620Z -               '    raise ValueError("Input too long")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539706Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1539959Z +               '# Declaring a minimum version to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540150Z +               'context = ssl.create_default_context()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540378Z +               'context.minimum_version = ssl.TLSVersion.TLSv1_2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540475Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540792Z -               "match = re.search(r'^(\\+|-)?(\\d+|(\\d*\\.\\d*))?(E|e)?([-+])?(\\d+)?$', str) \n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540877Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1540964Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1541078Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1541672Z +               '* Wikipedia: [ Transport Layer Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1542189Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ class ssl.SSLContext](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLContext).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.1542670Z +               '* Python 3 documentation: [ ssl.wrap_socket](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.wrap_socket).\n' +
