2026-10-20T21:06:14.2230082Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2230296Z +             markdown: '# URL redirection from remote source\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2231616Z +               'Directly incorporating user input into a URL redirect request without validating the input can facilitate phishing attacks. In these attacks, unsuspecting users can be redirected to a malicious site that looks very similar to the real site they intend to visit, but which is controlled by the attacker.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2231862Z -             markdown: '# Binding a socket to all network interfaces\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2234715Z -               'Sockets can be used to communicate with other machines on a network. You can use the (IP address, port) pair to define the access restrictions for the socket you create. When using the built-in Python `socket` module (for instance, when building a message sender service or an FTP server data transmitter), one has to bind the port to some interface. When you bind the port to all interfaces using `0.0.0.0` as the IP address, you essentially allow it to accept connections from any IPv4 address provided that it can get to the socket via routing. Binding to all interfaces is therefore associated with security risks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2234952Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2235045Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2235170Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2236235Z +               'To guard against untrusted URL redirection, it is advisable to avoid putting user input directly into a redirect URL. Instead, maintain a list of authorized redirects on the server; then choose from that list based on the user input provided.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2237206Z -               'Bind your service incoming traffic only to a dedicated interface. If you need to bind more than one interface using the built-in `socket` module, create multiple sockets (instead of binding to one socket to all interfaces).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2237296Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2238013Z +               'If this is not possible, then the user input should be validated in some other way, for example, by verifying that the target URL does not include an explicit host name.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2238097Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2238193Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2238298Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2238998Z +               'The following example shows an HTTP request parameter being used directly in a URL redirect without validating the input, which facilitates phishing attacks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2239701Z -               "In this example, two sockets are insecure because they are bound to all interfaces; one through the `0.0.0.0` notation and another one through an empty string `''`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2239783Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2239868Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2239969Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240179Z +               'from flask import Flask, request, redirect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240314Z -               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240394Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240544Z +               'app = Flask(__name__)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240727Z -               '# binds to all interfaces, insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2240970Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241121Z -               "s.bind(('0.0.0.0', 31137))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241203Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241462Z +               "@app.route('/')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241598Z +               'def hello():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241801Z +               "    target = request.args.get('target', '')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2241991Z +               '    return redirect(target, code=302)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2242172Z -               '# binds to all interfaces, insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2242407Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2242549Z -               "s.bind(('', 4040))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2242634Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2242841Z -               '# binds only to a dedicated interface, secure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2243078Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2243235Z -               "s.bind(('84.68.10.12', 8080))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2243325Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2243412Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2244241Z +               'If you know the set of valid redirect targets, you can maintain a list of them on the server and check that the user input is in that list:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2244475Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2244611Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2245084Z -               '* Python reference: [ Socket families](https://docs.python.org/3/library/socket.html#socket-families).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2245525Z -               '* Python reference: [ Socket Programming HOWTO](https://docs.python.org/3.7/howto/sockets.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2246027Z -               '* Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures: [ CVE-2018-1281 Detail](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2018-1281).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2246429Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-200](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2246659Z -             text: '# Binding a socket to all network interfaces\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2249326Z -               'Sockets can be used to communicate with other machines on a network. You can use the (IP address, port) pair to define the access restrictions for the socket you create. When using the built-in Python `socket` module (for instance, when building a message sender service or an FTP server data transmitter), one has to bind the port to some interface. When you bind the port to all interfaces using `0.0.0.0` as the IP address, you essentially allow it to accept connections from any IPv4 address provided that it can get to the socket via routing. Binding to all interfaces is therefore associated with security risks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2249423Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2249511Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2249656Z -               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2250628Z -               'Bind your service incoming traffic only to a dedicated interface. If you need to bind more than one interface using the built-in `socket` module, create multiple sockets (instead of binding to one socket to all interfaces).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2250717Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2250814Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2250943Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2251638Z -               "In this example, two sockets are insecure because they are bound to all interfaces; one through the `0.0.0.0` notation and another one through an empty string `''`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2251731Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2251824Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2251926Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2252134Z +               'from flask import Flask, request, redirect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2252260Z -               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2252344Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2252642Z +               'VALID_REDIRECT = "http://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2252821Z -               '# binds to all interfaces, insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2253204Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2253364Z -               "s.bind(('0.0.0.0', 31137))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2253448Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2253596Z +               'app = Flask(__name__)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2253774Z -               '# binds to all interfaces, insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254270Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254412Z -               "s.bind(('', 4040))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254492Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254632Z +               "@app.route('/')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254766Z +               'def hello():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2254964Z +               "    target = request.args.get('target', '')\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2255139Z +               '    if target == VALID_REDIRECT:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2255332Z +               '        return redirect(target, code=302)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2255454Z +               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2255698Z +               '        # ignore the target and redirect to the home page\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256035Z +               "        return redirect('/', code=302)\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256244Z -               '# binds only to a dedicated interface, secure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256483Z -               's = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256635Z -               "s.bind(('84.68.10.12', 8080))\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256722Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2256814Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2257940Z +               'Often this is not possible, so an alternative is to check that the target URL does not specify an explicit host name. For example, you can use the `urlparse` function from the Python standard library to parse the URL and check that the `netloc` attribute is empty.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2258028Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2258689Z +               'Note, however, that some cases are not handled as we desire out-of-the-box by `urlparse`, so we need to adjust two things, as shown in the example below:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2258820Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2259291Z -               '* Python reference: [ Socket families](https://docs.python.org/3/library/socket.html#socket-families).\n' +
