2026-10-20T21:06:14.2507447Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2507864Z +             text: 'Security checks on the substrings of an unparsed URL are often vulnerable to bypassing.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2508241Z -             text: 'Using broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithms can compromise security.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2508328Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2508410Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2508649Z +             markdown: '# Incomplete URL substring sanitization\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2509625Z +               'Sanitizing untrusted URLs is a common technique for preventing attacks such as request forgeries and malicious redirections. Usually, this is done by checking that the host of a URL is in a set of allowed hosts.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2510007Z -             markdown: '# Use of a broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithm on sensitive data\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2510596Z -               'Using a broken or weak cryptographic hash function can leave data vulnerable, and should not be used in security related code.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2510685Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2511756Z +               'However, treating the URL as a string and checking if one of the allowed hosts is a substring of the URL is very prone to errors. Malicious URLs can bypass such security checks by embedding one of the allowed hosts in an unexpected location.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2512179Z -               'A strong cryptographic hash function should be resistant to:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2512267Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2513033Z +               'Even if the substring check is not used in a security-critical context, the incomplete check may still cause undesirable behaviors when the check succeeds accidentally.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2513508Z -               '* pre-image attacks: if you know a hash value `h(x)`, you should not be able to easily find the input `x`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2514414Z -               '* collision attacks: if you know a hash value `h(x)`, you should not be able to easily find a different input `y` with the same hash value `h(x) = h(y)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2515644Z -               'In cases with a limited input space, such as for passwords, the hash function also needs to be computationally expensive to be resistant to brute-force attacks. Passwords should also have an unique salt applied before hashing, but that is not considered by this query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2515740Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2516112Z -               'As an example, both MD5 and SHA-1 are known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2516195Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2517072Z -               "Since it's OK to use a weak cryptographic hash function in a non-security context, this query only alerts when these are used to hash sensitive data (such as passwords, certificates, usernames).\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2517170Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2517819Z -               'Use of broken or weak cryptographic algorithms that are not hashing algorithms, is handled by the `py/weak-cryptographic-algorithm` query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2517918Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2518016Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2518139Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2518735Z +               'Parse a URL before performing a check on its host value, and ensure that the check handles arbitrary subdomain sequences correctly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519043Z -               'Ensure that you use a strong, modern cryptographic hash function:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519124Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519570Z -               '* such as Argon2, scrypt, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 for passwords and other data with limited input space.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519779Z -               '* such as SHA-2, or SHA-3 in other cases.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519861Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2519978Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2520564Z +               'The following example code checks that a URL redirection will reach the `example.com` domain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2521956Z -               'The following example shows two functions for checking whether the hash of a certificate matches a known value -- to prevent tampering. The first function uses MD5 that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks. The second function uses SHA-256 that is a strong cryptographic hashing function.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522061Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522148Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522255Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522474Z +               'from flask import Flask, request, redirect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522656Z +               'from urllib.parse import urlparse\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2522797Z -               'import hashlib\n' +
