2026-10-20T21:06:14.2540547Z -             text: '# Use of a broken or weak cryptographic hashing algorithm on sensitive data\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2541113Z -               'Using a broken or weak cryptographic hash function can leave data vulnerable, and should not be used in security related code.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2541204Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2542060Z +               'The second two examples show safe checks. In `safe1`, an allowlist is used. Although fairly inflexible, this is easy to get right and is most likely to be safe.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2542355Z -               'A strong cryptographic hash function should be resistant to:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2542449Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2542964Z +               'In `safe2`, `urlparse` is used to parse the URL, then the hostname is checked to make sure it ends with `.example.com`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2543417Z -               '* pre-image attacks: if you know a hash value `h(x)`, you should not be able to easily find the input `x`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2544316Z -               '* collision attacks: if you know a hash value `h(x)`, you should not be able to easily find a different input `y` with the same hash value `h(x) = h(y)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2545507Z -               'In cases with a limited input space, such as for passwords, the hash function also needs to be computationally expensive to be resistant to brute-force attacks. Passwords should also have an unique salt applied before hashing, but that is not considered by this query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2545621Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2546134Z -               'As an example, both MD5 and SHA-1 are known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2546222Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2546353Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2546703Z +               '* OWASP: [SSRF](https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2547454Z +               '* OWASP: [XSS Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2547854Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-20](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2548069Z +             text: '# Incomplete URL substring sanitization\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2549005Z +               'Sanitizing untrusted URLs is a common technique for preventing attacks such as request forgeries and malicious redirections. Usually, this is done by checking that the host of a URL is in a set of allowed hosts.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2549848Z -               "Since it's OK to use a weak cryptographic hash function in a non-security context, this query only alerts when these are used to hash sensitive data (such as passwords, certificates, usernames).\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2549942Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2550982Z +               'However, treating the URL as a string and checking if one of the allowed hosts is a substring of the URL is very prone to errors. Malicious URLs can bypass such security checks by embedding one of the allowed hosts in an unexpected location.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2551605Z -               'Use of broken or weak cryptographic algorithms that are not hashing algorithms, is handled by the `py/weak-cryptographic-algorithm` query.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2551702Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2552448Z +               'Even if the substring check is not used in a security-critical context, the incomplete check may still cause undesirable behaviors when the check succeeds accidentally.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2552539Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2552638Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2552765Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2553338Z +               'Parse a URL before performing a check on its host value, and ensure that the check handles arbitrary subdomain sequences correctly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2553648Z -               'Ensure that you use a strong, modern cryptographic hash function:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2553737Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2554417Z -               '* such as Argon2, scrypt, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 for passwords and other data with limited input space.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2554770Z -               '* such as SHA-2, or SHA-3 in other cases.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2554865Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2554981Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2555403Z +               'The following example code checks that a URL redirection will reach the `example.com` domain.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2556712Z -               'The following example shows two functions for checking whether the hash of a certificate matches a known value -- to prevent tampering. The first function uses MD5 that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks. The second function uses SHA-256 that is a strong cryptographic hashing function.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2556795Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2556883Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2556998Z                 '```python\n' +
