2026-10-20T21:06:14.2585365Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2585450Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2585738Z +             markdown: '# Construction of a cookie using user-supplied input\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2587805Z +               "Constructing cookies from user input can allow an attacker to control a user's cookie. This may lead to a session fixation attack. Additionally, client code may not expect a cookie to contain attacker-controlled data, and fail to sanitize it for common vulnerabilities such as Cross Site Scripting (XSS). An attacker manipulating the raw cookie header may additionally be able to set cookie attributes such as `HttpOnly` to insecure values.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2588032Z -             markdown: '# Use of insecure SSL/TLS version\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2588544Z -               'Using a broken or weak cryptographic protocol may make a connection vulnerable to interference from an attacker.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2588638Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2588719Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2588861Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2589085Z +               'Do not use raw user input to construct cookies.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2589865Z -               'Ensure that a modern, strong protocol is used. All versions of SSL, and TLS versions 1.0 and 1.1 are known to be vulnerable to attacks. Using TLS 1.2 or above is strongly recommended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2589955Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2590040Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2590280Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2591106Z +               "In the following cases, a cookie is constructed for a Flask response using user input. The first uses `set_cookie`, and the second sets a cookie's raw value through the `set-cookie` header.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2591730Z -               'The following code shows a variety of ways of setting up a connection using SSL or TLS. They are all insecure because of the version specified.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2591812Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2591899Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592010Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592210Z +               'from flask import request, make_response\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592339Z -               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592471Z -               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592559Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2592776Z -               '# Using the deprecated ssl.wrap_socket method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2593075Z -               'ssl.wrap_socket(socket.socket(), ssl_version=ssl.PROTOCOL_SSLv2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2593166Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2593313Z +               '@app.route("/1")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2593450Z +               'def set_cookie():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2593614Z +               '    resp = make_response()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2594347Z +               `    resp.set_cookie(request.args["name"], # BAD: User input is used to set the cookie's name and value\n` +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2594554Z +               '                    value=request.args["name"])\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2594694Z +               '    return resp\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2594832Z -               '# Using SSLContext\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595109Z -               'context = ssl.SSLContext(ssl_version=ssl.PROTOCOL_SSLv3)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595198Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595337Z -               '# Using pyOpenSSL\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595434Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595579Z +               '@app.route("/2")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595763Z +               'def set_cookie_header():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2595944Z +               '    resp = make_response()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2596524Z +               `    resp.headers['Set-Cookie'] = f"{request.args['name']}={request.args['name']};" # BAD: User input is used to set the raw cookie header.\n` +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2596654Z +               '    return resp\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2596821Z -               'from pyOpenSSL import SSL\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2596908Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597104Z -               'context = SSL.Context(SSL.TLSv1_METHOD)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597199Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597288Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597534Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597631Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2597990Z -               'All cases should be updated to use a secure protocol, such as `PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2598079Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2598516Z -               'Note that `ssl.wrap_socket` has been deprecated in Python 3.7. The recommended alternatives are:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2598608Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2598929Z -               '* `ssl.SSLContext` - supported in Python 2.7.9, 3.2, and later versions\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2599383Z -               '* `ssl.create_default_context` - a convenience function, supported in Python 3.4 and later versions.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2600622Z -               'Even when you use these alternatives, you should ensure that a safe protocol is used. The following code illustrates how to use flags (available since Python 3.2) or the \\`minimum_version\\` field (favored since Python 3.7) to restrict the protocols accepted when creating a connection.\n' +
