2026-10-20T21:06:14.2716077Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2716590Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [tempfile.mktemp](https://docs.python.org/3/library/tempfile.html#tempfile.mktemp).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2716998Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-377](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/377.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2717169Z +             text: '# Insecure temporary file\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2719380Z +               'Functions that create temporary file names (such as `tempfile.mktemp` and `os.tempnam`) are fundamentally insecure, as they do not ensure exclusive access to a file with the temporary name they return. The file name returned by these functions is guaranteed to be unique on creation but the file must be opened in a separate operation. There is no guarantee that the creation and open operations will happen atomically. This provides an opportunity for an attacker to interfere with the file before it is opened.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2719862Z -               '* Wikipedia: [ Transport Layer Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2720563Z -               '* Python 3 documentation: [ class ssl.SSLContext](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.SSLContext).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2721050Z -               '* Python 3 documentation: [ ssl.wrap_socket](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl.wrap_socket).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2721667Z -               '* Python 3 documentation: [ notes on context creation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#functions-constants-and-exceptions).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2722205Z -               '* Python 3 documentation: [ notes on security considerations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#ssl-security).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2722605Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-327](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2722827Z -             text: '# Default version of SSL/TLS may be insecure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2723589Z -               'The `ssl.wrap_socket` function defaults to an insecure version of SSL/TLS when no specific protocol version is specified. This may leave the connection vulnerable to attack.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2723683Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2724171Z +               'Note that `mktemp` has been deprecated since Python 2.3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2724406Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2724504Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2724627Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2725609Z +               'Replace the use of `mktemp` with some of the more secure functions in the `tempfile` module, such as `TemporaryFile`. If the file is intended to be accessed from other processes, consider using the `NamedTemporaryFile` function.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2727071Z -               'Ensure that a modern, strong protocol is used. All versions of SSL, and TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are known to be vulnerable to attacks. Using TLS 1.2 or above is strongly recommended. If no explicit `ssl_version` is specified, the default `PROTOCOL_TLS` is chosen. This protocol is insecure because it allows TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 and so should not be used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2727155Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2727238Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2727350Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2728229Z +               'The following piece of code opens a temporary file and writes a set of results to it. Because the file name is created using `mktemp`, another process may access this file before it is opened using `open`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2728931Z -               'The following code shows two different ways of setting up a connection using SSL or TLS. They are both potentially insecure because the default version is used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729015Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729094Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729207Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729373Z +               'from tempfile import mktemp\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729493Z -               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729625Z -               'import socket\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729707Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2729872Z +               'def write_results(results):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730030Z +               '    filename = mktemp()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730210Z +               '    with open(filename, "w+") as f:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730362Z +               '        f.write(results)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730561Z +               '    print("Results written to", filename)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730771Z -               '# Using the deprecated ssl.wrap_socket method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2730949Z -               'ssl.wrap_socket(socket.socket())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2731033Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2731170Z -               '# Using SSLContext\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2731333Z -               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2731423Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2731511Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2732048Z +               'By changing the code to use `NamedTemporaryFile` instead, the file is opened immediately.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2732732Z -               'Both of the cases above should be updated to use a secure protocol instead, for instance by specifying `ssl_version=PROTOCOL_TLSv1_2` as a keyword argument.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2732824Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2734624Z -               'The latter example can also be made secure by modifying the created context before it is used to create a connection. Therefore it will not be flagged by this query. However, if a connection is created before the context has been secured (for example, by setting the value of `minimum_version`), then the code should be flagged by the query `py/insecure-protocol`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2734712Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2735144Z -               'Note that `ssl.wrap_socket` has been deprecated in Python 3.7. The recommended alternatives are:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2735241Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2735555Z -               '* `ssl.SSLContext` - supported in Python 2.7.9, 3.2, and later versions\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2736001Z -               '* `ssl.create_default_context` - a convenience function, supported in Python 3.4 and later versions.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2737378Z -               'Even when you use these alternatives, you should ensure that a safe protocol is used. The following code illustrates how to use flags (available since Python 3.2) or the \\`minimum_version\\` field (favored since Python 3.7) to restrict the protocols accepted when creating a connection.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2737475Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2737569Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2737681Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2737878Z +               'from tempfile import NamedTemporaryFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738006Z -               'import ssl\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738092Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738249Z +               'def write_results(results):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738521Z +               '    with NamedTemporaryFile(mode="w+", delete=False) as f:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738674Z +               '        f.write(results)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2738861Z +               '    print("Results written to", f.name)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739053Z -               '# Using flags to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739209Z -               'context = ssl.SSLContext()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739461Z -               'context.options |= ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1 | ssl.OP_NO_TLSv1_1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739551Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739791Z -               '# Declaring a minimum version to restrict the protocol\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2739979Z -               'context = ssl.create_default_context()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2740213Z -               'context.minimum_version = ssl.TLSVersion.TLSv1_2\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2740303Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2740392Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2740476Z                 '\n' +
