2026-10-20T21:06:14.2939392Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2939896Z +             text: 'A regular expression that can require polynomial time to match may be vulnerable to denial-of-service attacks.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2940574Z -             text: 'Overly permissive regular expression ranges match a wider range of characters than intended. This may allow an attacker to bypass a filter or sanitizer.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2940658Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2940750Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2941055Z +             markdown: '# Polynomial regular expression used on uncontrolled data\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2942819Z +               'Some regular expressions take a long time to match certain input strings to the point where the time it takes to match a string of length *n* is proportional to *n<sup>k</sup>* or even *2<sup>n</sup>*. Such regular expressions can negatively affect performance, or even allow a malicious user to perform a Denial of Service ("DoS") attack by crafting an expensive input string for the regular expression to match.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2943088Z -             markdown: '# Overly permissive regular expression range\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2944460Z -               "It's easy to write a regular expression range that matches a wider range of characters than you intended. For example, `/[a-zA-z]/` matches all lowercase and all uppercase letters, as you would expect, but it also matches the characters: `` [ \\ ] ^ _ ` ``.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2944552Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2946819Z +               'The regular expression engine provided by Python uses a backtracking non-deterministic finite automata to implement regular expression matching. While this approach is space-efficient and allows supporting advanced features like capture groups, it is not time-efficient in general. The worst-case time complexity of such an automaton can be polynomial or even exponential, meaning that for strings of a certain shape, increasing the input length by ten characters may make the automaton about 1000 times slower.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2948372Z -               'Another common problem is failing to escape the dash character in a regular expression. An unescaped dash is interpreted as part of a range. For example, in the character class `[a-zA-Z0-9%=.,-_]` the last character range matches the 55 characters between `,` and `_` (both included), which overlaps with the range `[0-9]` and is clearly not intended by the writer.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2948461Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2949916Z +               'Typically, a regular expression is affected by this problem if it contains a repetition of the form `r*` or `r+` where the sub-expression `r` is ambiguous in the sense that it can match some string in multiple ways. More information about the precise circumstances can be found in the references.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2950015Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2950112Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2950233Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2951018Z +               'Modify the regular expression to remove the ambiguity, or ensure that the strings matched with the regular expression are short enough that the time-complexity does not matter.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2951864Z -               'Avoid any confusion about which characters are included in the range by writing unambiguous regular expressions. Always check that character ranges match only the expected characters.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2951946Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2952033Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2952150Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2952619Z +               'Consider this use of a regular expression, which removes all leading and trailing whitespace in a string:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953162Z -               'The following example code is intended to check whether a string is a valid 6 digit hex color.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953248Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953353Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953437Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953623Z +               're.sub(r"^\\s+|\\s+$", "", text) # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2953724Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2955631Z +               'The sub-expression `"\\s+$"` will match the whitespace characters in `text` from left to right, but it can start matching anywhere within a whitespace sequence. This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a whitespace character. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each whitespace sequence once per whitespace character in the sequence.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2955754Z -               'import re\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2955932Z -               'def is_valid_hex_color(color):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2956199Z -               "    return re.match(r'^#[0-9a-fA-f]{6}$', color) is not None\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2956279Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2957365Z +               'This ultimately means that the time cost of trimming a string is quadratic in the length of the string. So a string like `"a b"` will take milliseconds to process, but a similar string with a million spaces instead of just one will take several minutes.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2957462Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2958585Z +               'Avoid this problem by rewriting the regular expression to not contain the ambiguity about when to start matching whitespace sequences. For instance, by using a negative look-behind (`^\\s+|(?<!\\s)\\s+$`), or just by using the built-in strip method (`text.strip()`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2958685Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2959538Z +               'Note that the sub-expression `"^\\s+"` is **not** problematic as the `^` anchor restricts when that sub-expression can start matching, and as the regular expression engine matches from left to right.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2959633Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2959728Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2959858Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2960536Z +               'As a similar, but slightly subtler problem, consider the regular expression that matches lines with numbers, possibly written using scientific notation:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2960631Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2960760Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2960848Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2960997Z +               '^0\\.\\d+E?\\d+$ # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2961085Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2962167Z +               'The problem with this regular expression is in the sub-expression `\\d+E?\\d+` because the second `\\d+` can start matching digits anywhere after the first match of the first `\\d+` if there is no `E` in the input string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2962755Z -               'However, the `A-f` range is overly large and matches every uppercase character. It would parse a "color" like `#XXYYZZ` as valid.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2962842Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2964061Z +               'This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a digit. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each digit sequence once per digit in the sequence, again leading to a quadratic time complexity.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2964314Z -               'The fix is to use an uppercase `A-F` range instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2964401Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2965144Z +               'To make the processing faster, the regular expression should be rewritten such that the two `\\d+` sub-expressions do not have overlapping matches: `^0\\.\\d+(E\\d+)?$`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2965241Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2965496Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2965620Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2966923Z +               'Sometimes it is unclear how a regular expression can be rewritten to avoid the problem. In such cases, it often suffices to limit the length of the input string. For instance, the following regular expression is used to match numbers, and on some non-number inputs it can have quadratic time complexity:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2967019Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2967123Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2967208Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2967530Z +               "match = re.search(r'^(\\+|-)?(\\d+|(\\d*\\.\\d*))?(E|e)?([-+])?(\\d+)?$', str) \n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2967625Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2968688Z +               'It is not immediately obvious how to rewrite this regular expression to avoid the problem. However, you can mitigate performance issues by limiting the length to 1000 characters, which will always finish in a reasonable amount of time.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2968827Z -               'import re\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2968999Z -               'def is_valid_hex_color(color):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969260Z -               "    return re.match(r'^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$', color) is not None\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969342Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969468Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969562Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969700Z +               'if len(str) > 1000:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969894Z +               '    raise ValueError("Input too long")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2969989Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2970295Z +               "match = re.search(r'^(\\+|-)?(\\d+|(\\d*\\.\\d*))?(E|e)?([-+])?(\\d+)?$', str) \n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2970388Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2970474Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2970587Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2971199Z +               '* OWASP: [Regular expression Denial of Service - ReDoS](https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2971471Z +               '* Wikipedia: [ReDoS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ReDoS).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2971833Z +               '* Wikipedia: [Time complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_complexity).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2972512Z +               '* James Kirrage, Asiri Rathnayake, Hayo Thielecke: [Static Analysis for Regular Expression Denial-of-Service Attack](https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0849).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2972929Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-1333](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1333.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2973469Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-730](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/730.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2974121Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-400](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2974421Z +             text: '# Polynomial regular expression used on uncontrolled data\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2976206Z +               'Some regular expressions take a long time to match certain input strings to the point where the time it takes to match a string of length *n* is proportional to *n<sup>k</sup>* or even *2<sup>n</sup>*. Such regular expressions can negatively affect performance, or even allow a malicious user to perform a Denial of Service ("DoS") attack by crafting an expensive input string for the regular expression to match.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2976994Z -               '* GitHub Advisory Database: [CVE-2021-42740: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command in Shell-quote](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g4rg-993r-mgx7)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2977495Z -               '* wh0.github.io: [Exploiting CVE-2021-42740](https://wh0.github.io/2021/10/28/shell-quote-rce-exploiting.html)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2977999Z -               '* Yosuke Ota: [no-obscure-range](https://ota-meshi.github.io/eslint-plugin-regexp/rules/no-obscure-range.html)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2978479Z -               '* Paul Boyd: [The regex \\[,-.\\]](https://pboyd.io/posts/comma-dash-dot/)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2978876Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-20](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2979107Z -             text: '# Overly permissive regular expression range\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2980209Z -               "It's easy to write a regular expression range that matches a wider range of characters than you intended. For example, `/[a-zA-z]/` matches all lowercase and all uppercase letters, as you would expect, but it also matches the characters: `` [ \\ ] ^ _ ` ``.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2980307Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2982555Z +               'The regular expression engine provided by Python uses a backtracking non-deterministic finite automata to implement regular expression matching. While this approach is space-efficient and allows supporting advanced features like capture groups, it is not time-efficient in general. The worst-case time complexity of such an automaton can be polynomial or even exponential, meaning that for strings of a certain shape, increasing the input length by ten characters may make the automaton about 1000 times slower.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2984341Z -               'Another common problem is failing to escape the dash character in a regular expression. An unescaped dash is interpreted as part of a range. For example, in the character class `[a-zA-Z0-9%=.,-_]` the last character range matches the 55 characters between `,` and `_` (both included), which overlaps with the range `[0-9]` and is clearly not intended by the writer.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2984441Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2985722Z +               'Typically, a regular expression is affected by this problem if it contains a repetition of the form `r*` or `r+` where the sub-expression `r` is ambiguous in the sense that it can match some string in multiple ways. More information about the precise circumstances can be found in the references.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2985811Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2985912Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2986042Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2986820Z +               'Modify the regular expression to remove the ambiguity, or ensure that the strings matched with the regular expression are short enough that the time-complexity does not matter.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2987640Z -               'Avoid any confusion about which characters are included in the range by writing unambiguous regular expressions. Always check that character ranges match only the expected characters.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2987875Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2987962Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2988077Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2988552Z +               'Consider this use of a regular expression, which removes all leading and trailing whitespace in a string:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2988969Z -               'The following example code is intended to check whether a string is a valid 6 digit hex color.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2989057Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2989172Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2989251Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2989448Z +               're.sub(r"^\\s+|\\s+$", "", text) # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2989547Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2991175Z +               'The sub-expression `"\\s+$"` will match the whitespace characters in `text` from left to right, but it can start matching anywhere within a whitespace sequence. This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a whitespace character. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each whitespace sequence once per whitespace character in the sequence.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2991429Z -               'import re\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2991606Z -               'def is_valid_hex_color(color):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2991865Z -               "    return re.match(r'^#[0-9a-fA-f]{6}$', color) is not None\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2991951Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2993025Z +               'This ultimately means that the time cost of trimming a string is quadratic in the length of the string. So a string like `"a b"` will take milliseconds to process, but a similar string with a million spaces instead of just one will take several minutes.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2993116Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2994534Z +               'Avoid this problem by rewriting the regular expression to not contain the ambiguity about when to start matching whitespace sequences. For instance, by using a negative look-behind (`^\\s+|(?<!\\s)\\s+$`), or just by using the built-in strip method (`text.strip()`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2994642Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2995483Z +               'Note that the sub-expression `"^\\s+"` is **not** problematic as the `^` anchor restricts when that sub-expression can start matching, and as the regular expression engine matches from left to right.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2995580Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2995673Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2995797Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2996474Z +               'As a similar, but slightly subtler problem, consider the regular expression that matches lines with numbers, possibly written using scientific notation:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2996571Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2996691Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2996787Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2996934Z +               '^0\\.\\d+E?\\d+$ # BAD\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2997023Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2997953Z +               'The problem with this regular expression is in the sub-expression `\\d+E?\\d+` because the second `\\d+` can start matching digits anywhere after the first match of the first `\\d+` if there is no `E` in the input string.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2998520Z -               'However, the `A-f` range is overly large and matches every uppercase character. It would parse a "color" like `#XXYYZZ` as valid.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2998607Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2999601Z +               'This is problematic for strings that do **not** end with a digit. Such a string will force the regular expression engine to process each digit sequence once per digit in the sequence, again leading to a quadratic time complexity.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2999835Z -               'The fix is to use an uppercase `A-F` range instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.2999922Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3000803Z +               'To make the processing faster, the regular expression should be rewritten such that the two `\\d+` sub-expressions do not have overlapping matches: `^0\\.\\d+(E\\d+)?$`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3000906Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3001003Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3001129Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3002421Z +               'Sometimes it is unclear how a regular expression can be rewritten to avoid the problem. In such cases, it often suffices to limit the length of the input string. For instance, the following regular expression is used to match numbers, and on some non-number inputs it can have quadratic time complexity:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3002520Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3002631Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3002711Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3003027Z +               "match = re.search(r'^(\\+|-)?(\\d+|(\\d*\\.\\d*))?(E|e)?([-+])?(\\d+)?$', str) \n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3003131Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3004387Z +               'It is not immediately obvious how to rewrite this regular expression to avoid the problem. However, you can mitigate performance issues by limiting the length to 1000 characters, which will always finish in a reasonable amount of time.\n' +
