2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480109Z -               '    x = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480268Z -               '    [0 for x in range(3)]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480536Z -               '    return x # Will return 2 in Python 2 and 3 in Python 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480621Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480781Z +               '    def __init__(self, data):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3480936Z +               '        self.data = data\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3481084Z -               'print(two_or_three())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3481178Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3481767Z -               'The following example is the same code as above, but the list comprehension variable is renamed to ensure it does not overwrite `x`.\n' +
