2026-10-20T21:06:14.3510971Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3511084Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3511598Z +             text: 'A lambda is used that calls through to a function without modifying any parameters'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3512094Z -             text: 'Capturing a loop variable is not the same as capturing its value, and can lead to unexpected behavior or bugs.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3512181Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3512260Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3512427Z +             markdown: '# Unnecessary lambda\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3513283Z +               'A lambda that calls a function without modifying any of its parameters is unnecessary. Python functions are first class objects and can be passed around in the same way as the resulting lambda.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3513455Z -             markdown: '# Loop variable capture\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3514723Z -               'In Python, a nested function or lambda expression that captures a variable from its surrounding scope is a *late-binding* closure, meaning that the value of the variable is determined when the closure is called, not when it is created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3514823Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3515875Z -               'Care must be taken when the captured variable is a loop variable. If the closure is called after the loop ends, it will use the value of the variable on the last iteration of the loop, rather than the value at the iteration at which it was created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3516107Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3516202Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3516320Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3516542Z +               'Remove the lambda, use the function directly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3517527Z -               "Ensure that closures that capture loop variables aren't used outside of a single iteration of the loop. To capture the value of a loop variable at the time the closure is created, use a default parameter, or `functools.partial`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3517606Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3517689Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3517807Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3518332Z +               'In this example a lambda is used unnecessarily in order to pass a method as an argument to ` call_with_x_squared`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3518941Z -               'In the following (BAD) example, a `tasks` list is created, but each task captures the loop variable `i`, and reads the same value when run.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519026Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519105Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519227Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519360Z +               'import math\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519567Z -               '# BAD: The loop variable `i` is captured.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519695Z -               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3519844Z -               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520024Z -               '    tasks.append(lambda: print(i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520112Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520303Z +               'def call_with_x_squared(x, function):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520437Z +               '    x = x*x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520593Z +               '    return function(x)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520686Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3520963Z +               'print call_with_x_squared(2, lambda x: math.factorial(x))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3521281Z -               '# This will print `4,4,4,4,4`, rather than `0,1,2,3,4` as likely intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3521416Z -               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3521540Z -               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3521631Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3522028Z +               'This is not necessary as methods can be passed directly. They behave as callable objects.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3523138Z -               'In the following (GOOD) example, each closure has an `i` default parameter, shadowing the outer `i` variable, the default value of which is determined as the value of the loop variable `i` at the time the closure is created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3523231Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3523312Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3523426Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3523559Z +               'import math\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524197Z -               '# GOOD: A default parameter is used, so the variable `i` is not being captured.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524338Z -               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524483Z -               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524686Z -               '    tasks.append(lambda i=i: print(i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524776Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3524969Z +               'def call_with_x_squared(x, function):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525096Z +               '    x = x*x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525247Z +               '    return function(x)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525413Z -               '# This will print `0,1,2,3,4``.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525556Z -               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525675Z -               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3525776Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3526349Z -               'In the following (GOOD) example, `functools.partial` is used to partially evaluate the lambda expression with the value of `i`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3526572Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3526801Z +               'print call_with_x_squared(2, math.factorial)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3526898Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3527019Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3527161Z -               'import functools\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3527490Z -               '# GOOD: `functools.partial` takes care of capturing the _value_ of `i`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3527613Z -               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3527757Z -               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528026Z -               '    tasks.append(functools.partial(lambda i: print(i), i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528128Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528301Z -               '# This will print `0,1,2,3,4``.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528431Z -               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528556Z -               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528649Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3528730Z                 '\n' +
