2026-10-20T21:06:14.3612800Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3614415Z +               'It is not good practice to compare a value with itself, as it makes the code hard to read and can hide errors with classes that do not correctly implement equality. If testing whether a floating-point value is not-a-number, then use `math.isnan()`. If the value may be a complex number, then use `cmath.isnan()` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3615102Z -               'Ensure that the second assignment is in fact correct. Then delete the first assignment (taking care not to delete right hand side if it has side effects).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3615191Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3615272Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3615388Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3616115Z +               "In this example `f == f` is used to check for `float('nan')`. This makes the code difficult to understand as the reader may not be immediately familiar with this pattern.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3616917Z -               'In this example, `x` is assigned the value of 42 but then the value is changed to 12 before `x` is used. This makes the first assignment useless.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617007Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617094Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617199Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617294Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617542Z +               "#Using 'x == x' to check that 'x' is not a float('nan').\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617684Z +               'def is_normal(f):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3617927Z +               '    return not cmath.isinf(f) and f == f\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3618020Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3618226Z +               '#Improved version; intention is explicit.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3618369Z +               'def is_normal(f):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3618599Z +               '    return not cmath.isinf(f) and not cmath.isnan(f)\n' +
