2026-10-20T21:06:14.3695686Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3695903Z +             markdown: '# Wrong number of arguments in a call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3696381Z +               'A function call must supply an argument for each parameter that does not have a default value defined, so:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3696743Z -             markdown: '# Module-level cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3698402Z -               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement. If all imports in a cyclic import occur at module level, then a module will be imported when it is part way through its initialization. This may rest in surprising errors, as parts of the module being imported may not yet exist.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3698492Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3698877Z +               '* The minimum number of arguments is the number of parameters without default values.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3699568Z +               '* The maximum number of arguments is the total number of parameters, unless the function takes a varargs (starred) parameter in which case there is no limit.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3700598Z -               'In addition to the possible errors, cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3700687Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3700777Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3700903Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3701393Z +               'If there are too few arguments then check to see which arguments have been omitted and supply values for those.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3702812Z -               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3702896Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3703342Z +               'If there are too many arguments then check to see if any have been added by mistake and remove those.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3703426Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3704299Z +               'Also check where a comma has been inserted instead of an operator or a dot. For example, the code is `obj,attr` when it should be `obj.attr`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3704402Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3705056Z +               'If it is not clear which are the missing or surplus arguments, then this suggests a logical error. The fix will then depend on the nature of the error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3705145Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3705239Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3705351Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3705901Z +               '* Python Glossary: [Arguments](https://docs.python.org/2/glossary.html#term-argument).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3706306Z +               '* Python Glossary: [Parameters](https://docs.python.org/glossary.html#term-parameter).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3707032Z +               '* Python Programming FAQ: [ What is the difference between arguments and parameters?](https://docs.python.org/2/faq/programming.html#faq-argument-vs-parameter).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3707434Z +               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-685](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/685.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3707641Z +             text: '# Wrong number of arguments in a call\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3708108Z +               'A function call must supply an argument for each parameter that does not have a default value defined, so:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3708627Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3708940Z -               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3709479Z -               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3709792Z -             text: '# Module-level cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3711464Z -               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement. If all imports in a cyclic import occur at module level, then a module will be imported when it is part way through its initialization. This may rest in surprising errors, as parts of the module being imported may not yet exist.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3711551Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3711926Z +               '* The minimum number of arguments is the number of parameters without default values.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3712616Z +               '* The maximum number of arguments is the total number of parameters, unless the function takes a varargs (starred) parameter in which case there is no limit.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3713633Z -               'In addition to the possible errors, cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3713719Z                 '\n' +
