2026-10-20T21:06:14.3727578Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3727884Z +             text: 'Comparison using `is` when equivalence is not the same as identity'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3728223Z -             text: 'Module forms part of an import cycle, thereby indirectly importing itself.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3728305Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3728387Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3728672Z +             markdown: '# Comparison using is when operands support `__eq__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3730774Z +               'When you compare two values using the `is` or `is not` operator, it is the object identities of the two values that is tested rather than their equality. If the class of either of the values in the comparison redefines equality then the `is` operator may return `False` even though the objects compare as equal. Equality is defined by the `__eq__` or, in Python2, `__cmp__` method. To compare two objects for equality, use the `==` or `!=` operator instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3730946Z -             markdown: '# Cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3731641Z -               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3731728Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3732592Z -               'Cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3732679Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3732773Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3733025Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3733554Z +               'When you want to compare the value of two literals, use the comparison operator `==` or `!=` in place of `is` or `is not`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3735271Z -               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3735358Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3735841Z +               'If the uniqueness property or performance are important then use an object that does not redefine equality.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3735932Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3736023Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3736155Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3737080Z +               'In the first line of the following example the programmer tests the value of `value` against `DEFAULT` using the `is` operator. Unfortunately, this may fail when the function is called with the string `"default"`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3737171Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738126Z +               'To function correctly, change the expression `value is DEFAULT` to `value == DEFAULT`. Alternatively, if the uniqueness property is desirable, then change the definition of `DEFAULT` to either of the alternatives below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738221Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738311Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738434Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738522Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738667Z +               'DEFAULT = "default"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738766Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3738934Z +               'def get_color(name, fallback):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739087Z +               '    if name in COLORS:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739250Z +               '        return COLORS[name]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739410Z +               '    elif fallback is DEFAULT:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739569Z +               '        return DEFAULT_COLOR\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739693Z +               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739838Z +               '        return fallback\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3739931Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740053Z +               '#This works\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740263Z +               'print (get_color("spam", "def" + "ault"))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740363Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740503Z +               '#But this does not\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740733Z +               'print (get_color("spam", "default-spam"[:7]))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3740972Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741174Z +               '#To fix the above code change to object\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741317Z +               'DEFAULT = object()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741410Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741600Z +               '#Or if you want better repr() output:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741754Z +               'class Default(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3741843Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742025Z +               '    def __repr__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742183Z +               '        return "DEFAULT"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742274Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742414Z +               'DEFAULT = Default()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742509Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742603Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742696Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3742809Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3743294Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Comparisons](http://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#comparisons).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3743561Z +             text: '# Comparison using is when operands support `__eq__`\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3745861Z +               'When you compare two values using the `is` or `is not` operator, it is the object identities of the two values that is tested rather than their equality. If the class of either of the values in the comparison redefines equality then the `is` operator may return `False` even though the objects compare as equal. Equality is defined by the `__eq__` or, in Python2, `__cmp__` method. To compare two objects for equality, use the `==` or `!=` operator instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3746400Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3746723Z -               '* Python: [Modules](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/modules.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3747266Z -               '* Effbot: [Import Confusion](https://web.archive.org/web/20200917011425/https://effbot.org/zone/import-confusion.htm).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3747411Z -             text: '# Cyclic import\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3748101Z -               'A cyclic import is an `import` which imports another module and that module imports (possibly indirectly) the module which contains the `import` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3748190Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3749055Z -               'Cyclic imports indicate that two modules are circularly dependent. This means that the modules cannot be tested independently, and it makes it harder to understand the architecture of the system.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3749144Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3749240Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3749361Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3749890Z +               'When you want to compare the value of two literals, use the comparison operator `==` or `!=` in place of `is` or `is not`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3751314Z -               'The cycle may be broken by removing any one import. If only one function or method requires the import, then consider moving that to the other module and deleting the import. If the two modules are more intimately connected, then move the inter-dependent parts into a third module and have both the original modules import that.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3751398Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3751878Z +               'If the uniqueness property or performance are important then use an object that does not redefine equality.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3751962Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3752050Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3752179Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3753231Z +               'In the first line of the following example the programmer tests the value of `value` against `DEFAULT` using the `is` operator. Unfortunately, this may fail when the function is called with the string `"default"`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3753333Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3754511Z +               'To function correctly, change the expression `value is DEFAULT` to `value == DEFAULT`. Alternatively, if the uniqueness property is desirable, then change the definition of `DEFAULT` to either of the alternatives below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3754611Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3754707Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3754832Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3754920Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755065Z +               'DEFAULT = "default"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755160Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755329Z +               'def get_color(name, fallback):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755481Z +               '    if name in COLORS:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755638Z +               '        return COLORS[name]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755800Z +               '    elif fallback is DEFAULT:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3755963Z +               '        return DEFAULT_COLOR\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756080Z +               '    else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756230Z +               '        return fallback\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756472Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756596Z +               '#This works\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756802Z +               'print (get_color("spam", "def" + "ault"))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3756895Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757031Z +               '#But this does not\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757250Z +               'print (get_color("spam", "default-spam"[:7]))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757340Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757538Z +               '#To fix the above code change to object\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757678Z +               'DEFAULT = object()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757768Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3757957Z +               '#Or if you want better repr() output:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758111Z +               'class Default(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758200Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758349Z +               '    def __repr__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758505Z +               '        return "DEFAULT"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758595Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758736Z +               'DEFAULT = Default()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758824Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3758924Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3759024Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3759136Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3759603Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Comparisons](http://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#comparisons).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3760120Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The import statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#import).\n' +
