2026-10-20T21:06:14.3840188Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3840445Z +             markdown: '# Unmatchable dollar in regular expression\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3842052Z +               'A dollar assertion `$` in a regular expression only matches at the end of the input, or (for multi-line regular expressions) at the end of a line. If it is followed by a pattern that must match a non-empty sequence of (non-newline) input characters, it cannot possibly match, rendering the entire regular expression unmatchable.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3842217Z -             markdown: '# Syntax error\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3842916Z -               'Syntax errors prevent a module being evaluated and thus imported. An attempt to import a module with invalid syntax will fail; a `SyntaxError` will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3843005Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3844542Z -               'A common cause of syntax errors is the difference in syntax between Python 2 and Python 3. In particular, a syntax error may be alerted if a Python 3 file is assumed to be compatible with Python 2 (or vice versa). Explicitly specifying the expected Python version can help prevent this.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3844640Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3844784Z +               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3845070Z +               'Examine the regular expression to find and correct any typos.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3846058Z -               'The existence of a syntax error in a module may suggest other problems as well. Either the module is never imported in practice and could be deleted or a `try` statement around the import is mistakenly discarding the `SyntaxError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3846142Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3846227Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3846349Z +               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3847194Z +               'In the following example, the regular expression `r"\\.\\(\\w+$\\)"` cannot match any string, since it contains a dollar assertion followed by an escape sequence that matches a closing parenthesis.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3847337Z -               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3848080Z -               'Fixing the syntax error is the obvious fix. However, it is worth investigating why a module containing a syntax error was able to persist and address that problem as well.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3848168Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3848599Z +               'The second regular expression, `r"\\.\\(\\w+\\)$"`, has the dollar at the end and will work as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3850963Z -               'If you suspect that the syntax error is caused by the analysis using the wrong version of Python, consider specifying the version explicitly. When you run code scanning using the CodeQL action, you can configure the Python version to use. For more information, see [Analyzing Python dependencies](https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/automatically-scanning-your-code-for-vulnerabilities-and-errors/configuring-code-scanning#analyzing-python-dependencies).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851061Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851163Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851290Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851419Z +               'import re\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851715Z +               '#Regular expression that includes a dollar, but not at the end.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3851910Z +               'matcher = re.compile(r"\\.\\(\\w+$\\)")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3852047Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.3852471Z -               '* Python Tutorial: [SyntaxErrors.](http://docs.python.org/tutorial/errors.html#syntax-errors)\n',
